Implementation: for each constructor c and downstream d, compute the transitive closure of constants reachable from d's type+value (using value? true), and check whether c ∈ closure. To avoid infinite loops, BFS with a visited set. Cost: for each of 30 pairs, BFS over the proof graph — could be large but manageable if restricted (e.g. only traverse constants declared in package modules, not all of mathlib; and cap). Actually the constructors are project constants, so I can traverse only project-module constants (packageRoots), which keeps it bounded.

Let me write TransitProbe.lean:
```lean
run_cmd do
  let env ← getEnv
  let mods := env.header.moduleNames
  let inPkg (n : Name) : Bool := ...  -- module of n is under packageRoots
  for (ctor, down) in pairs do
    match env.find? down with
    | none => print missing
    | some ci =>
      let mut seen : Array Name := #[]
      let mut stack : Array Name := ci.type.getUsedConstants ++ (ci.value? true).map (·.getUsedConstants) |>.getD #[]
      let mut found := false
      while !stack.isEmpty do
        let x := stack.back; stack := stack.pop
        if x == ctor then found := true; stack := #[]
        else if seen.contains x then pure ()
        else
          seen := seen.push x
          if inPkg x then
            match env.find? x with
            | some c2 =>
              stack := stack ++ (c2.type.getUsedConstants ++ ((c2.value? true).map (·.getUsedConstants) |>.getD #[]))
            | none => pure ()
      IO.println s!"D13XTRANS\t{ctor}\t{down}\t{found}"
```
Careful with Array API (`.back`, `.pop` exist? In Lean 4.34: `Array.back?`, `Array.pop`). Let me use lists for simplicity: collect `List Name`, use recursion with a fuel limit.

Simpler: implement recursively with `partial def`? Avoid partial. Use a worklist `Array Name` with index:
```lean
let mut work : Array Name := initial
let mut i := 0
let mut seen : Array Name := #[]
let mut found := false
while i < work.size do
  let x := work[i]!
  ...
  i := i + 1
```
`Array.size`, `work[i]!` works. Appending `work := work ++ extra` inside loop is fine.

Also cap iterations (e.g. i < 200000) to avoid runaway.

